  "summary": "Recent preprints and publications dissect how histone and DNA methylation marks interface with Polycomb silencing during development and chromatin maintenance.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how chromatin modifications and their regulators establish or restrict repressive states during gene regulation. In mouse embryonic stem cells, H3K4 methylation and DNA 5-methylcytosine cooperate to shield regulatory elements from Polycomb silencing. In mouse cortex, EHMT1 and EHMT2 set the timing of neurogenesis through both H3K9me2-dependent and H3K9me2-independent mechanisms, shaping progenitor fate and neuron subtype output. In Neurospora crassa, the CAF-1 histone chaperone is required for facultative heterochromatin structure and PRC2-dependent gene silencing. In Xenopus embryos, maternal Foxh1 recruits PRC2/Ezh2 to specific loci, shaping H3K27me3 and germ layer lineage segregation. Together these studies place locus-specific deposition and maintenance of repressive marks at the center of developmental and genomic control.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
